<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canute Lavard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1096">1096</a>–1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Knud Lavard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canute Lavard was a Danish prince, son of King Eric I, who became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> around 1115. He defended the border against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> and gained authority among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a> as their prince. His popularity alarmed his cousin Magnus, who murdered him in 1131, sparking civil war. He was canonised in 1170, and his son became King Valdemar I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canute-lavard.jpg" alt="Canute Lavard" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FredrikT · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knut_Lavard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Canute Lavard, born on 12 March 1096, was the only legitimate son of King Eric I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, who died in 1103, and Boedil Thurgotsdatter. As a minor he was bypassed in the election of 1104. He grew up in close contact with the noble family of Hvide, who later became among his most eager supporters. His byname Lavard is cognate with the English Lord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1115 his uncle, King Niels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, placed him in charge of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> in order to end the attacks of the Slavic Obotrites. Over the next fifteen years he established peace in the border area so well that he was titled Duke of Holstein and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. He was the first Duke of Schleswig and the first border prince who was both a Danish and a German vassal, a position leading toward the later double position of Southern Jutland.</p><p>He seems to have been the first member of the Danish royal family attracted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> ideals of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and he changed his title to Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>. His appearance made him popular and a possible successor to Niels, but he acquired mighty enemies among Danish princes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, who apparently questioned his loyalty and feared his bond with Emperor Lothair III, who had recognised him as sovereign over the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>.</p><p>He married Ingeborg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-i-of-kiev">Mstislav I of Kiev</a> and Christina Ingesdotter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. They had four children: Margaret, Christina, Catherine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark">Valdemar I of Denmark</a>, born in 1131.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Niels and his son Magnus the Strong seem to have been alarmed by the emperor's recognition. On 7 January 1131 Canute was trapped in the Haraldsted Forest near Ringsted on Zealand and murdered by Magnus. Some sources consider the death a murder by Magnus, others attribute it to Niels himself. Ringsted Abbey became his first resting place, and in 1157 his remains were moved to a new chapel at St. Bendt's Church in Ringsted.</p><p>After his death the Obotrite lands were partitioned between the chiefs Pribislav and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Niklot</a>, and a civil war continued intermittently until 1157, ending with the triumph of his posthumous son Valdemar I. At Valdemar's request Canute was canonised in 1170. His feast day, Knutsdagen, was originally 7 January and later moved to 13 January. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canute-guild">Canute Guild</a> in Tallinn was named in his memory, and a 4-metre crucifix was raised near the site of his death in 1902.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canute Lavard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 11th century it was governed as a jarldom or duchy by members of the royal family; <b>Canute Lavard</b> held it from 1115, and the dukes descended from King Abel made it a hereditary fief.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-schleswig">Duchy of Schleswig</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Knud Lavards Chapel, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.64,12.08&amp;year=1118" aria-label="Roskilde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4814 266 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Canute Lavard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5264 536h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5264 536h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.64,12.08&amp;year=1118">What was here in 1118? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
